modern role-playing games didn't just  arise from the digital ashes no they  stand on the shoulders of adventures  like Ultima 7 I have the beholder  Baldur's Gate and Planescape torment  developer obsidian entertainment  well-known for RPGs ranging from Knights  of the Old Republic to to Alpha Protocol  knows its history and with pillars of  eternity it hopes to recapture a piece  of it whether or not pillars of eternity  has the same magic as Baldur's Gate the  game which it clearly looks upon with  reverence isn't yet clear several  members of the game's development team  showed off the game to me recently and  it only took a single glance to see that  pillars of eternity is based on  Bioware's classic it doesn't take place  in the Dungeons &amp; Dragons universe but  pillars nonetheless looks and feels like  its inspiration the environments have  that hand-drawn grittiness that gave the  infinity engine games an air of  fantastical despair selected party  members are identified with pulsing blue  circles under their feet and combat is  in that pause and play style that serves  as Boulder's gates signature as I  watched obsidian play pillars of  eternity it was hard not to shake the  concern that stayed with me throughout  the demo that this was not a game  standing on the shoulders of giants but  one relying on slavish devotion it  didn't help that I just played the  excellent divinity original sin an RPG  that uses its inspirations as a  springboard rather than as an exact mold  this pillars of eternity use old  mechanics to say something old and if so  is that necessarily a bad thing pillars  of eternity project director Josh Sawyer  doesn't think so we chatted a bit about  the baldur's gate legacy and the ways  obsidian plans to differentiate their  game from its forebears and four Sawyer  a big part of that is rethinking how  classes function for instance rogues are  the most offensively powerful weapon  based characters that's a change from  2nd and 3rd edition D&amp;D where they are  mostly skill based the demo included  pillars of eternity z' character  creation which  me to get a good look at the games  classes in addition to its fantasy races  a hora is an original world and while  some of its races are familiar enough a  few of them captured my imagination the  godlike race in particular intrigues me  the godlike I saw looked to be wearing a  tall pointed headpiece made of tendons  and cartilage though apparently this is  no headpiece but rather a part of the  God likes Anatomy the gameplay I saw was  comforting and familiar as the Barbarian  and his companions ventured into the  nearby ruins there was one modern twist  however events were described as they  occurred by handsome hand-drawn story  panels story decisions aren't relegated  just to these panels however frenching  conversation trees also play a role in  the continuing story and like with most  games some of those options might be  available only if you've reached a  certain threshold and skills like  honesty diplomacy and passion but while  you can usually assume that unlocked  options are always the right answer in  other role-playing games that's not  always the case in pillars of eternity  you'll need to pay attention to the  conversation as well as to the context  it appears in the demo closed when the  adventuring party emerged into a nearby  ruins where a giant warring magical  device awaited them I was left wondering  if pillars of eternity will refresh  role-playing traditions or simply  replicate them and whether pure  replication is a bad thing at all  nevertheless I have faith not just in  the strength of the Infinity engine  template but in obsidians ability to use  that template to craft a journey worth  taking
 

